WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.410
BRENNAN SAETA: TPUs
are making an excuse

00:00:02.410 --> 00:00:05.830
of "my model is training"
a thing of the past.

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:08.109
Looking to learn a bit
more about how they work?

00:00:08.109 --> 00:00:09.850
Well, you've come
to the right place.

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:12.550
With over 500
sessions at Next 2018,

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:14.950
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:17.500
So if you didn't see
"Programming ML Supercomputers:

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.080
A Deep Dive on Cloud
TPUs," then stay tuned,

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:22.435
because here's the rewind.

00:00:22.435 --> 00:00:30.080
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:32.630
This session was a
real treat, a chance

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:36.830
to hear about Tensor Processing
Units, or TPUs, directly

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:39.200
from a member of the
Google Brain team.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.870
The session was broken up
into three main parts--

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:44.150
the life of a TPU program,
which talks about what

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:47.210
happens under the hood
when TPU programs are run,

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:49.460
writing a TPU program,
which talks about the API

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:52.070
for writing TPU
code, and, finally,

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:55.580
feeding a TPU program,
which is discussing how

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:59.300
to get your data into the TPU
fast enough to really utilize

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:02.560
its powerful
number-crunching abilities.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.750
TPUs were made to combat the
exponentially-rising amount

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:08.380
of compute required to train
state-of-the-art machine

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.350
learning models in recent years.

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:11.920
And it's iterating pretty quick.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:15.650
TPUv3 has already come out,
barely a year after version 2.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:19.970
TPU chips have a powerful
matrix multiplier at their core.

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:22.700
And in the TPU version 2,
which is available, today,

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:27.680
as Cloud TPUs, there are four
TPU chips on a single board.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.560
Now, these boards are
then arranged into pods

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:33.560
of 64 TPU boards each.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:35.510
Today, you can get
access to a TPU

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:37.970
with just a couple
of GCloud commands.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:40.190
And actually, writing
code from the TPU

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:43.220
involves using some wrappers
around the TensorFlow estimator

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:45.470
API, called TPUEstimators.

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:48.050
These wrappers help convert
your existing TensorFlow program

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:50.100
into something that
can be run on the TPU

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:53.000
utilizing the TF to XLA bridge.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.680
There are some
limitations, of course,

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:57.230
such as requiring static
shapes for your arrays

00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:00.080
and only being able to use
a subset of the operations

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.000
that TensorFlow can express.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.450
But in exchange, you
get terrific speed.

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:06.440
How fast?

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:08.210
It's fast enough that,
if you hook it up

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:10.550
to an SSD hard
drive, the hard drive

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:13.460
won't be able to supply
the data fast enough.

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:16.340
So instead, we turn to
high-performance distributed

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:17.390
systems.

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:19.526
One option is Google
Cloud Storage.

00:02:19.526 --> 00:02:20.900
You want to use
regional buckets,

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:23.390
with your data converted
to TFRecord format,

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:27.020
and the file split into
one to 200 megabyte chunks.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:29.000
Another option is
to use Bigtable,

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.460
which has great performance,
straight-out-of-the-box,

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:34.900
up to 10x that of Cloud
Storage, sometimes.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:37.290
TPUs, they're pretty amazing.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.470
They're like time
machines in ways, right?

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:41.330
Instead of waiting
for days or weeks,

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:44.240
even, you can iterate
in hours or faster.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:48.830
This supercharges ML research as
well as ML product development.

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:51.020
And as we continue to bring
you these improvements,

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:54.560
in compute power, storage
capability, and networking,

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:56.150
the sky is the limit.

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:58.350
So develop your
applications ethically,

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:00.490
conduct your
research responsibly,

00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:04.340
and I am super excited to
see what you build with TPUs.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:06.200
Thanks for watching
this Cloud "Rewind."

